@* The house's House Way and the ranking of the hands, as one table: used
   by Hand Setting's Help (PaiGowCombined) and the Pay Tables tool's House
   Way tab (ToolPayTables). One copy, so the two never disagree. *@

<table class="help-table sp-help-table hw-table">
    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Two Pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>No Ace: split only if one pair is 7s or better.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>With an Ace: split only if Jacks or better AND 7s or better.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Always split Aces and any other pair.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Three Pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Always play the highest pair in front.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Three of a Kind</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Always keep three of a kind together, unless they are Aces; then play a pair of Aces in back and one Ace in front.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>If the dealer has 3 Aces and at the same time has a straight or a flush, the straight or flush will be played.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Straights, Flushes, Straight Flushes and Royal Flushes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Keep as the high hand, except:</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>a</b>6 and 7 card: play the low straight or flush to place the highest card in front.</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>b</b>Play a straight over a flush anytime the second highest hand will be improved. This includes straight flushes and royal flushes.</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>c</b>Play two pair (apply the two pair rule) except when both of the pairs are 6s or below.</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>d</b>With a Full House, apply the Full House rules.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Full House</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Always split, playing the pair in front, unless the pair is 2s and you have an Ace and a King to play in front.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>With an extra pair, play the higher of the two pairs in front.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Four of a Kind</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Always keep 6s and under together.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Always split 7s through 10s, playing a pair in each hand, unless the hand contains an Ace or better to play in front.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Always split Jacks through Kings, playing a pair in each hand.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Split four Aces unless with another pair:</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>a</b>If the other pair is 6s or below, play Aces in front, two pair in back.</td></tr>
    <tr class="hw-sub"><td></td><td><b>b</b>If the other pair is 7s or above, play four Aces in back, pair in front.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Five Aces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Split and play three in back and two in front, unless the hand also contains a pair of Kings; then play 5 Aces in back and Kings in front.</td></tr>

    <tr class="sp-pt-hd"><td colspan="2">Ranking of the Hands (highest first)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Five Aces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Royal Flush</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Straight Flush</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>Four of a Kind</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Full House</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Flush</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>Straight (the wheel, A-2-3-4-5, is the 2nd highest)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>Three of a Kind</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>Two Pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>One Pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>High Card</td></tr>
</table>
